# Data

This is where the workbook gets its content: a named set of leads the rest of the
app enriches, scores, and ranks. It's also the lesson on the **data model**.

A type is **structure, not a DSL**. You write a `defstruct`; the field types are
inferred from the defaults — `name: ""` is a string, `revenue: 0` an integer,
`status: :new` an atom. That shape *is* the type. The lifecycle is an **enum** — a
closed set of atoms:

@statuses [:new, :enriched, :scored, :contacted]

A data source is **typed values of that struct**. `of:` names the record; `from:
values([...])` puts the rows right in the document — no sidecar DB, no JSON, no CSV.
The document is the store.

data :leads, of: Lead, from: values([
  %Lead{name: "Acme",    domain: "acme.co",     revenue: 50_000, employees: 20, score: 70, status: :scored},
  %Lead{name: "Globex",  domain: "globex.io",   revenue: 12_000, employees: 8,  score: 41, status: :enriched},
  %Lead{name: "Initech", domain: "initech.com", revenue: 90_000, employees: 55, score: 88, status: :scored}
])

A data source **renders by default**. Declaring `:leads` is enough to put a typed
table on the page — headers come from the struct fields, the `status` atoms render
as chips. The value *is* the view; you write no markup.

Next: pulling a slice with [[Query & show]].
